id":"route-decision:benguela","entity_id":"current:benguela","label":"Benguela Current","current_id":"benguela","strategy_id":"source_gated_regional_route","strategy_label":"Establish a sourced regional route","construction_status":"editorial_candidate_present_scientific_review_pending","candidate_count":1,"route_ids":["benguela-reference-path-candidate"],"next_action":"Review the existing editorial route against scientific gates; broader/system/family scope remains governed by the planning strategy.","scope":"The study identifies the Benguela Current system at 30°, 24°, and 19°S across decadal inverse solutions, with no continuity north of 19°S. The current and Benguela ecosystem have different extents. Section observations are from different cruises and do not establish a simultaneous centerline; the derived floor is unranked.","status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","rank_eligible":false,"source_url":"https://www.vos.noaa.gov/docs/marine_info_guide.pdf","source_decision":{"current_id":"benguela","name":"Benguela Current","existing_length_evidence_status":"derived_lower_bound","existing_scope":"The study identifies the Benguela Current system at 30°, 24°, and 19°S across decadal inverse solutions, with no continuity north of 19°S. The current and Benguela ecosystem have different extents. Section observations are from different cruises and do not establish a simultaneous centerline; the derived floor is unranked.","name_source_url":"https://www.vos.noaa.gov/docs/marine_info_guide.pdf","candidate_ids":["benguela-reference-path-candidate"],"reference_path_decision":"editorial_candidate_present_scientific_review_pending","published_length_rank_eligible":false,"ledger_kind":"eastern boundary current system","route_strategy_id":"source_gated_regional_route","route_strategy_label":"Establish a sourced regional route","planning_status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","planning_basis":"The study identifies the Benguela Current system at 30°, 24°, and 19°S across decadal inverse solutions, with no continuity north of 19°S. The current and Benguela ecosystem have different extents. Section observations are from different cruises and do not establish a simultaneous centerline; the derived floor is unranked.","next_action":"Review the existing editorial route against scientific gates; broader/system/family scope remains governed by the planning strategy.","strategy_next_action":"Read primary geographic definitions, choose explicit endpoint/branch/layer/time conventions, and measure a reviewed route with alternatives; naming points alone are insufficient.","known_component_currents":[],"component_inventory_status":"not_assessed_by_this_planning_crosswalk"},"source_catalog_file":"research/ocean-current-reference-path-candidates.json","source_catalog_sha256":"b06caed27655c39f8127ddda441cba0a856800f599ec52edcd5b30d2ad37b633","scope_reviews":[]},{"id":"route-decision:black-sea-rim","entity_id":"current:black-sea-rim","label":"Black Sea Rim Current","current_id":"black-sea-rim","strategy_id":"dated_geometry","strategy_label":"Construct dated or regime-specific geometry","construction_status":"editorial_candidate_present_scientific_review_pending","candidate_count":1,"route_ids":["black-sea-rim-reference-path-candidate"],"next_action":"Review the existing editorial route against scientific gates; broader/system/family scope remains governed by the planning strategy.","scope":"Copernicus Marine defines this basin-scale cyclonic flow and measures intensity in northern and southern slope sectors. Those sectors are monitoring windows, not a full current centerline or length. Older Caucasus and Southern Current names may describe sectors, but equivalence is unresolved.","status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","rank_eligible":false,"source_url":"https://data.marine.copernicus.eu/product/OMI_CIRCULATION_BOUNDARY_BLKSEA_rim_current_index/description","source_decision":{"current_id":"black-sea-rim","name":"Black Sea Rim Current","existing_length_evidence_status":"no_numeric_length","existing_scope":"Copernicus Marine defines this basin-scale cyclonic flow and measures intensity in northern and southern slope sectors. Those sectors are monitoring windows, not a full current centerline or length. Older Caucasus and Southern Current names may describe sectors, but equivalence is unresolved.","name_source_url":"https://data.marine.copernicus.eu/product/OMI_CIRCULATION_BOUNDARY_BLKSEA_rim_current_index/description","candidate_ids":["black-sea-rim-reference-path-candidate"],"reference_path_decision":"editorial_candidate_present_scientific_review_pending","published_length_rank_eligible":false,"ledger_kind":"cyclonic basin-margin current system","route_strategy_id":"dated_geometry","route_strategy_label":"Construct dated or regime-specific geometry","planning_status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","planning_basis":"Copernicus Marine defines this basin-scale cyclonic flow and measures intensity in northern and southern slope sectors. Those sectors are monitoring windows, not a full current centerline or length. Older Caucasus and Southern Current names may describe sectors, but equivalence is unresolved.","next_action":"Review the existing editorial route against scientific gates; broader/system/family scope remains governed by the planning strategy.","strategy_next_action":"Use dated or explicitly averaged geometry, distinguish an open loop from a closed circuit, and compare repeated regimes before summarizing.","known_component_currents":[],"component_inventory_status":"not_assessed_by_this_planning_crosswalk"},"source_catalog_file":"research/ocean-current-reference-path-candidates.json","source_catalog_sha256":"b06caed27655c39f8127ddda441cba0a856800f599ec52edcd5b30d2ad37b633","scope_reviews":[{"note":{"current_id":"black-sea-rim","label":"Black Sea closed-circuit scope and state exclusions","audit_file":"research/black-sea-rim-circuit-scope-audit.json","summary":"Declared closed reference circuit excludes interior gyres, coastal eddy loops and seasonal continuity claims. MEDI/REDS raw display contacts are geographic mask artifacts, retained in the audit and excluded from navigation.","source_url":"https://os.copernicus.org/articles/10/155/2014/os-10-155-2014.pdf","source_locator":"Introduction; Section 2; Sections 3.1 and 3.2.1: surface geostrophic context, seasonal anomalies and Rim circuit around interior gyres.","observed_period":null},"document":{"schema":"osw.current-source-scope-audit.v1","current_id":"black-sea-rim","as_of":"2026-10-03","status":"editorial_source_scope_audit_not_canonical","source_url":"https://os.copernicus.org/articles/10/155/2014/os-10-155-2014.pdf","source_access":"primary_publisher_pdf_relevant_intro_methods_and_sections_3_1_3_2_read","source_citation":"Menna and Poulain (2014), Geostrophic currents and kinetic energies in the Black Sea estimated from merged drifter and satellite altimetry data, Ocean Science 10, 155-165, doi:10.5194/os-10-155-2014.","source_locator":"Introduction; Section 2; Sections 3.1 and 3.2.1: surface geostrophic context, seasonal anomalies and Rim circuit around interior gyres.","source_license":"Publisher PDF states CC Attribution 3.0. No source figures, trajectories or velocity grids packaged.","layer":"Surface geostrophic circulation context; source drifters have mixed near-surface support through the first 15 m, with wind-driven components estimated separately. No uniform full-depth axis inferred.","sampling_context":"1999-2009 study with 89 drifters combined with altimetry; seasonal and interannual statistics.","observed_period":null,"topology_decision":"Declared counterclockwise closed reference circuit, with arbitrary repeated anchor rather than current origin or termination. Not a continuously observed parcel circuit.","seasonal_continuity":"Source describes stronger winter Rim flow and weaker/more fragmented summer-fall circulation. Closed reference geography does not establish continuity in every month.","excluded_objects":["Interior Western and Eastern gyres","Coastal mesoscale eddies and their loops","Northern/southern Copernicus intensity monitoring windows treated as full-current endpoints"],"naming_overlap":"Caucasus and Southern Current historical-name equivalence remains unresolved; no alias admission.","whole_current_length_km":null,"whole_current_width_km":null,"annual_length_range_km":null,"annual_width_range_km":null,"next_action":"Review source-period convention, bathymetry and seasonal continuity before canonical admission; closed circuit candidate now available.","candidate_file":"research/black-sea-rim-reference-path-candidate.json","input_file":"research/black-sea-rim-reference-path-input.json","reference_circuit_measurement":{"approximate_km":2100,"scenario_range_km":[1900,2200],"scenarios":9,"physical_axis_diagnosed":false},"state_scope_note":"The Black Sea has no dedicated state in this 56-state display inventory. Raw MEDI/REDS polygon contacts are coarse-shape artifacts, retained in the audit and excluded from state navigation; they do not place the circuit in the Mediterranean or Red Sea."},"source_sha256":"9c58a1ec293970d8e55d59a14a40c42c5369163f00af21d12cc4a847087c09ba"}]},{"id":"route-decision:brazil","entity_id":"current:brazil","label":"Brazil Current","current_id":"brazil","strategy_id":"source_gated_regional_route","strategy_label":"Establish a sourced regional route","construction_status":"editorial_candidate_present_scientific_review_pending","candidate_count":1,"route_ids":["brazil-reference-path-candidate"],"next_action":"Review the existing editorial route against scientific gates; broader/system/family scope remains governed by the planning strategy.","scope":"The study identifies southward Brazil Current transport at 19°, 24°, and 30°S. Confluence location and downstream identity vary. Secti